WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.140
Hi!

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:05.629
We have learned in the previous short that
most CNNs do not actually compute convolutions,

00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:07.899
but the cross-correlation instead.

00:00:07.899 --> 00:00:09.440
Why should we care?

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.990
We should care if we want to answer this follow-up
Quiz question on this.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.800
Now seriously, in our last short we already
mentioned a reason: porting models from one

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:23.009
library using the cross-correlation to another
one using the convolution (or vice-versa),

00:00:23.009 --> 00:00:28.339
can be a headache without knowing that learned
kernels are rotated by 180 degrees.

00:00:28.339 --> 00:00:33.670
Otherwise, while many of the convolution properties
are kept, the commutativity of the convolution

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:39.190
is lost, since we also lost the kernel flipping
in the convolution that was ensuring commutativity.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.949
Ms. Coffee Bean just wrote a mini example
down for you.

00:00:41.949 --> 00:00:43.600
Pause the video if needed.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:49.120
Admittedly losing commutativity by replacing
convolutions through cross-correlations is

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.710
nothing that bothers Machine Learning people.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:56.480
While commutativity is important for writing
mathematical proofs, it’s not relevant when

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:57.530
training your CNN.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:58.250
Okay, bye!

